From c6d5ba89cfcc3ba2777040d7bddbe27ac38d4fa5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 09:19:35 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] amigaos: loop distribution stays on by default

0006 added -fno-tree-loop-distribute-patterns to the driver's defaults.
With 0004 (movmemsi copies overlapping blocks the way memmove does) loop
distribution is correct, and turning it off cost real speed: on the AC090 a
shift-up loop that would be one memmove ran about 3 times slower as a loop
(3.0 to 3.8 times in repeated runs), a sorted insert 1.2 to 1.3 times.

The default now protects only null-pointer checks and calloc. Code that
defines its own memcpy, memmove, memset or calloc as a loop (a C library)
builds with -fno-tree-loop-distribute-patterns itself; libnix's build does
(scripts/build.sh).
---
 gcc/config/m68k/m68kamigaos.h | 13 ++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gcc/config/m68k/m68kamigaos.h b/gcc/config/m68k/m68kamigaos.h
index 57b04eb..8364a95 100644
--- a/gcc/config/m68k/m68kamigaos.h
+++ b/gcc/config/m68k/m68kamigaos.h
@@ -773,10 +773,13 @@ amigaos_prelink_hook((const char **)(LD1_ARGV), (STRIP))
      null checks that follow a read.
    - A program's own calloc written as malloc + memset must not become a call
      to calloc (itself, or a calloc paired with a different malloc).
-   - Loop distribution turns loops into memset/memcpy/memmove calls, which
-     recurse inside a program's own mem* functions and smeared overlapping
-     "shift up" loops before the movmemsi fix.  */
+   Loop distribution (-ftree-loop-distribute-patterns) is NOT turned off: with
+   the movmemsi fix a "shift up" loop becomes a correct overlapping memmove, and
+   the pass is worth a lot of speed on a 68k (a shift loop is several times
+   faster as a block move).  Code that defines its own memcpy, memmove, memset
+   or calloc as a loop (a C library) must be built with
+   -fno-tree-loop-distribute-patterns itself, or its loop becomes a call to
+   itself; libnix's build does.  */
 #define OPENAMIGAGCC_SAFE_DEFAULTS \
   "%{!fdelete-null-pointer-checks:-fno-delete-null-pointer-checks}", \
-  "%{!fmalloc-memset-to-calloc:-fno-malloc-memset-to-calloc}", \
-  "%{!ftree-loop-distribute-patterns:-fno-tree-loop-distribute-patterns}"
+  "%{!fmalloc-memset-to-calloc:-fno-malloc-memset-to-calloc}"
